The '''2007 election''' for the federal [[Parliament of Australia|Parliament]] of [[Australia]] will take place on [[Saturday]] [[24 November]]. The [[Prime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]] [[John Howard]] announced the date on [[14 October]].<ref>[http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/10/14/2059084.htm?section=justin ''PM announces November 24 poll''], [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC Online]], [[14 October]] [[2007]].</ref>

The [[Opposition (Australia)|opposition]] [[centre-left]] [[Australian Labor Party]], currently led by [[Kevin Rudd]] and deputy leader [[Julia Gillard]], will be the main challenger to the [[incumbent]] [[centre-right]] [[Coalition (Australia)|coalition]] government in power since the [[Australian federal election, 1996|1996 election]], currently led by the Prime Minister and leader of the [[Liberal Party of Australia]], John Howard, and his coalition partner the [[Deputy Prime Minister of Australia|Deputy Prime Minister]] and leader of the [[National Party of Australia]], [[Mark Vaile]].

Following the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]] outcome, Labor, with 60 seats, will need a swing of at least 16 additional seats in the 150-member [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] to form a [[majority government]].

As well as the coalition and Labor, a number of minor parties and independents have a realistic chance of winning seats in the [[Australian Senate|Senate]], in which the coalition currently has a 1-seat majority.

==Key dates==
* [[Prorogation]] of Parliament: 12 p.m., 15 October<ref>[http://www.ag.gov.au/portal/govgazonline.nsf/C2592AB524E7B3A0CA2573750007F345/$file/S204.pdf]</ref>
* [[Dissolution of parliament|Dissolution]] of House of Representatives: 12 p.m., 17 October<ref>[http://www.ag.gov.au/portal/govgazonline.nsf/6B2E00A5A0304DEDCA25737700048846/$file/S208.pdf]</ref>
* Issue of electoral [[writ]]s: 17 October
* Close of [[Electoral roll|rolls]] (if not currently on roll): 8 p.m., 17 October<ref>{{cite press release | title = Statement from the Australian Electoral Commission regarding enrolment deadlines for the 2007 federal election | publisher = Australian Electoral Commission | date = [[2007-10-14]] | url = http://www.aec.gov.au/About_AEC/Media_releases/10_14a.htm | accessdate = 2007-10-15 }}</ref>
* Close of rolls (if currently on roll and updating details): 8 p.m., 23 October
* Close of nominations: 12 noon, 1 November
* Declaration of nominations: 12 noon, 2 November
* Polling Day: 24 November<ref>{{cite web | title = 2007 Federal Election Timetable | publisher = Australian Electoral Commission | url = http://www.aec.gov.au/Elections/federal_elections/2007/Timetable.htm | accessdate = 2007-10-15 }}</ref><ref>{{cite press release | title = Federal election 2007 announced | publisher = Australian Electoral Commission | date = [[2007-10-14]] | url = http://www.aec.gov.au/About_AEC/Media_releases/10_14.htm | accessdate = 2007-10-15 }}</ref>
* Return of writs: On or before 25 January 2008<ref>[http://www.ag.gov.au/portal/govgazonline.nsf/443DE0650D78221ACA257377001D52AE/$file/S210.pdf]</ref>
* First meeting of the 42nd Parliament: On or before 24 February 2008<ref>[http://scaleplus.law.gov.au/html/pasteact/1/641/0/PA000200.htm]</ref>
* New Senators begin their terms: 1 July 2008

Under the provisions of the [[Constitution of Australia|Constitution]], the current House of Representatives may continue for a maximum of three years from the first meeting of the House after the previous general election. The first meeting of the 41st Parliament after the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]] was on [[16 November]] [[2004]], hence the parliament would have expired on [[15 November]] [[2007]] had it not been dissolved earlier. There must be a minimum of 33 days and a maximum of 68 days between the dissolution of the House of Representatives and the day of the election.<ref> [http://www.aph.gov.au/library/handbook/elections/index.htm#introduction]</ref> Prime Minister Howard opted for a 39-day campaign.

The Prime Minister of the day chooses the election date and requests the [[Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]] to dissolve the House and issue the writs for the election. On [[14 October]], John Howard gained the agreement of the Governor-General, Major-General [[Michael Jeffery]], to dissolve the House of Representatives and hold a general election for the House and half the Senate on [[24 November]] [[2007]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Election looms on November 24|url=http://www.theage.com.au/news/national/election-on-november-24/2007/10/14/1192300571760.html|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[2007-10-14]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

==Polling==
[[Image:Pollchart-ppm.svg|left|thumb|250px|Preferred Prime Minister polling since the [[Australian federal election, 2004|previous election]] by [[Newspoll]] (as published in ''[[The Australian]]'') [http://www.newspoll.com.au/cgi-bin/polling//display_poll_data.pl?mode=trend&page=continue_results&question_id=2422&url_caller=trend 1987-2007], and Preferred Prime Minister polling by [[ACNielsen]] [http://au.acnielsen.com/news/200512.shtml 1996-2007].]][[Image:Pollchart-tpp-event.svg|right|thumb|250px|[[Mackerras Pendulum|Two party preferred]] polling since the [[Australian federal election, 2004|previous election]] by [[Newspoll]] (as published in [[The Australian]]) [http://www.newspoll.com.au/cgi-bin/polling/display_poll_data.pl?mode=trend&page=continue_results&question_id=3488&url_caller= 1993-2007], [[ACNielsen]] [http://au.acnielsen.com/news/200512.shtml 1996-2007], [[Roy Morgan Research|Roy Morgan]] [http://www.roymorgan.com/news/polls/Trends.cfm? 1996-2007], and [[Galaxy Research|Galaxy]] [http://www.ozpolitics.info/blog/category/galaxy 2004-2007].]]
[http://www.ozpolitics.info/guide/elections/fed2007/polls2007/ Roy Morgan, Newspoll, ACNeilsen and Galaxy timegraph polling] shows Labor leading the coalition in responses to a number of key poll questions since mid-2006, with Labor consolidating its lead after Rudd assumed the Labor leadership, at which point Rudd also assumed the polling position on the question of preferred Prime Minister.

[[ACNielsen]] polling in March 2007 had Rudd's personal approval rating at 67%, which made him the most popular opposition leader in the poll's 35-year history,<ref name="smh_acnielsen_070312">{{cite news|first=Phillip|last=Coorey|title=Ruddslide: polls shows Labor increasing its lead|url=http://www.smh.com.au/news/national/its-going-to-be-a-ruddslide/2007/03/11/1173548022964.html|work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|date=[[2007-03-12]]|accessdate=2007-03-12}}</ref> with [[Newspoll]] ([[News Limited]]) 2PP polling the highest in its history. The largest 2PP election result for the ALP in its history was at the [[Australian federal election, 1943|1943 election]] on an estimate of 58.2 percent.

A weighted collaboration of all polling since Rudd assumed the ALP leadership shows an average Labor 2PP figure of 57% compared with the coalition's 43%,<ref>[http://www.theage.com.au/news/federalelection2007news/measuring-the-public-mood--do-numbers-ever-lie/2007/10/15/1192300611904.html The Age]</ref> and Rudd's consistent outpolling of Howard as preferred Prime Minister, something not achieved under previous leaders [[Mark Latham]], [[Kim Beazley]] or [[Simon Crean]].

Possums Pollytics states that due to the uneven nature of the swings, where safe Liberal seats are swinging up to 14.6% with safe Labor seats swinging around only 4.1%, the Labor party stands to potentially end up with a maximum of 106 of the 150 lower house seats.<ref>http://possumcomitatus.wordpress.com/</ref>

Despite polling consistently showing that the economy and national security are the Coalition's strong areas, August 2007 saw Ipsos polling revealing 39% of voters thought Labor was the better economic manager, compared with 36% for the coalition, while 25% were undecided.<ref>{{cite news|title=Government shrugs off poll|url=http://www.smh.com.au/news/national/government-shrugs-off-poll/2007/08/26/1188066918904.html|work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|date=[[2007-08-26]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

The morning of the election announcement, a special Sun-Herald Taverner survey of 979 people across New South Wales and Victoria had been released, indicating a Labor 2PP of 59 percent, with the 18-29 year old category voting at 72 percent.<ref>{{cite news|first=Kerry-Anne|last=Walsh|title=PM's enemies: The young and the restless|url=http://www.smh.com.au/news/federalelection2007news/howards-enemies-the-young-and-the-restless/2007/10/13/1191696235752.html|work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|date=[[2007-10-13]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> The fortnightly Newspoll was released the day after the election was called, showing the 2PP remaining steady at Labor 56-44 Liberal. Howard increased his Preferred PM rating up one percent to 39 percent, while Rudd increased his rating up one percent to 48 percent. On the day after the election was called, [[Centrebet]] had odds of A$1.47 on Labor, with A$2.70 on the coalition.<ref>{{cite news|first=Tim|last=Colebatch|title=Where the election will be won and lost|url=http://www.theage.com.au/news/federalelection2007news/where-the-election-will-be-won-and-lost/2007/10/15/1192300612363.html?page=fullpage#contentSwap1|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[2007-10-15]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

==Issues==
[[Image:WorkChoices Logo.PNG|left|thumb|The most comprehensive changes to Australia's [[industrial relations]] system since federation, known as [[WorkChoices]], were enacted by the Howard Liberal government in December 2005, and came in to effect in March 2006.]][[Image:RecordLows.JPG|right|thumb|Electoral advertising shown on Australian free-to-air and pay TV in 2004 by the [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]] indicating the commitment of a Howard Liberal government to "keep interest rates at record lows". Labor has criticised this after five [[interest rate]] rises since the [[Australian federal election, 2004|October 2004 federal election]]. In response, Mr Howard stated in August 2007 "[Rudd] can scour every transcript, and I will make them available, of every interview that I gave during that election campaign and he will find no such commitment."<ref>http://www.aph.gov.au/hansard/reps/dailys/dr080807.pdf Questions without notice, Page 46</ref> In October 2007, Mr Howard "admitted breaking a promise to keep interest rates at record lows".<ref>http://www.news.com.au/heraldsun/story/0,21985,22653762-5005961,00.html</ref>]]

Roy Morgan polling in June 2007 revealed [[WorkChoices]] as the biggest reason behind the Labor vote, with a fear of union dominance and support for coalition economic management policy as the biggest reasons behind the coalition vote.<ref name="issues1">{{cite web |title=IR Reforms Still Driving Labor Support; Liberal Voters Afraid Of Union Dominance |publisher=Roy Morgan Research |date=[[2007-06-18]] |url=http://www.roymorgan.com/news/polls/2007/4179/ |accessdate=2007-07-07}}</ref> Attempts by the Liberal Party to have business groups fund advertisements to counter union funded anti-WorkChoices advertisements suggest that industrial relations will be a key battleground at the 2007 election.<ref>{{cite web |last=Coorey |first=Phillip |title=PM's appeal to business shows workplace ads failed: Labor |publisher=The Sydney Morning Herald |date=[[2007-04-05]] |url=http://www.smh.com.au/news/national/pms-appeal-to-business-shows-workplace-ads-failed-labor/2007/04/05/1175366414240.html |accessdate=2007-04-15}}</ref> The share of voters concerned about industrial relations grew from 31% to 53% in the two years to June 2006, with around three fifths of voters backing Labor's ability to handle the issue over the Liberal Party.<ref name="issues"/> 

A Newspoll released in June 2006 identified health and [[Medicare (Australia)|Medicare]] as the most important issue for voters, with 83% of respondents rating it "very important". Other key issues included education (79%), the economy (67%), the environment (60%) and national security (60%). Taxation and interest rates, key issues in previous campaigns, were rated very important by 54% and 51% respectively. Immigration, a key issue in [[Australian federal election, 2001|2001]], scored 43%. The poll showed that voters considered Labor better-placed to handle health and education, albeit by a small margin, but gave the government strong backing on the economy and national security.<ref name="issues">{{cite web |title=Importance and best party to handle major issues |publisher=Newspoll/The Australian |date=[[2006-06-06]] |url=http://www.newspoll.com.au/image_uploads/0604%20issues.pdf |accessdate=2007-04-15}}</ref>

The environment, in particular [[climate change]] and water management, will likely be a major issue. Labor's pledge to spearhead construction of a $4.7 billion [[fiber to the node|fibre-to-the-node]] broadband network suggests that infrastructure could also figure prominently on the campaign trail.<ref>{{cite news|last=Barker|first=Gary|title=Broadband looms as an election issue|url=http://www.theage.com.au/news/business/broadband-looms-as-an-election-issue/2007/03/08/1173166895703.html|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[2007-03-09]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

On 7 June in a speech promoting the government's handling of the economy, Treasurer Peter Costello recalled the [[:Image:Ac.liberalad.jpg|learner driver slogan]] of the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]]: "This [the economy] is like a highly engineered racing car and I tell you what, I wouldn't be putting an L-plate driver in the cockpit at the moment".<ref>{{cite news|title=Costello warns against economic 'L-plater'|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/newsitems/200706/s1945363.htm|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=[[2007-06-07]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> August 2007 saw a 0.25% [[interest rate]] rise to 6.5%, the fifth rise since the last election. Labor used the news to argue that the Coalition could not be trusted to keep interest rates low, while Costello argued that interest rates would be higher under Labor.<ref>{{cite news|title=Interest rates jump to 6.5pc|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/08/08/1999347.htm|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=[[2007-08-08]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> Inflation figures released on 24 October indicated underlying inflation was higher than expected, which resulted in seven of eight financial companies (ie: [[JPMorgan]]) believing there will be an interest rate rise when the reserve bank meets in the middle of November, which if rates rose would be the first during an election campaign.<ref>[http://www.theage.com.au/news/federalelection2007news/families-under-pressure/2007/10/24/1192941122824.htm Families under pressure, The Age]</ref><ref>[http://www.news.com.au/heraldsun/story/0,21985,22640212-5005961,00.html Herald Sun]</ref>

Rudd has advocated four-year fixed terms for federal parliaments if elected. Howard supports four-year terms but opposes fixed election dates. Any change would require approval by referendum.<ref>{{cite news|first=Phillip|last=Coorey|title=10 ministers face wipeout: poll warning|url=http://www.smh.com.au/news/national/10-ministers-face-oblivion--polls/2007/10/05/1191091364601.html|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[2007-10-06]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> Another referendum was suggested by John Howard on the eve of the election campaign, in order to include a statement of reconciliation to be included in the preamble of the constitution.<ref>{{cite news|title=PM plans reconciliation referendum|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/10/11/2057292.htm|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=[[2007-10-11]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

==The campaign==

===Week 1 - leadership and tax===
John Howard announced the election at a press conference in [[Canberra]] on 14 October, after meeting with the Governor-General at [[Government House, Canberra|Government House]]. His theme concentrated on leadership, stating that the nation ''"does not need new leadership, it does not need old leadership. It needs the right leadership"''. He also stated that his government would strive to achieve full employment, something he argued was less likely to eventuate under Kevin Rudd.<ref>http://www.news.com.au/story/0,23599,22581572-5012863,00.html</ref> In his response, Rudd also concentrated on leadership, outlining his case for "new leadership”. He argued that the government had 'lost touch' with the electorate, and that his party was best suited to deal with challenges that lie ahead.

On the first full day of the campaign, Howard and Costello announced a 'major restructuring of the income tax system' with tax cuts worth $35 billion over three years and a tax cut "goal" for the next five years.<ref>[http://www.smh.com.au/news/economy/pledge-to-cut-tax/2007/10/15/1192300657771.html Pledge to cut tax, Syndey Morning Herald]</ref> A few days later, Rudd released his alternative policy which supported the reform measures, however offered education and health tax rebates instead of cuts to the top rate as proposed by the Liberal Party.<ref>http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/story/0,25197,22613427-601,00.html</ref>

During the latter part of the week attention was drawn to the question of union influence over the ALP after the launch of the Liberal party's first campaign ads. Labor responded with commercials attacking the Liberal's campaign as 'smears'. This was disputed by John Howard. One of the Liberal Party election commercials had to be corrected after it falsely accused [[Wayne Swan]] and [[Craig Emerson]] of previously being union officials.<ref>http://au.news.yahoo.com/071017/2/14pc3.html</ref>

The week saw contradictory poll results in terms of the scale of Labor's lead. Galaxy showed a Labor 53-47 Coalition 2PP result, with a 2 percent gap on primaries,<ref>{{cite news|first=Gerard|last=McManus|title=John Howard narrows poll gap on Kevin Rudd|url=http://www.news.com.au/heraldsun/story/0,21985,22610163-661,00.html|work=[[Herald Sun]]|date=[[2007-10-19]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> while ACNeilsen saw a two percent swing to the coalition, reducing Labor's lead to 54-46. Rudd took a five-point dive on the question of preferred Prime Minister. However, a Newspoll sampling 1700 voters taken over the weekend prior to the debate saw a swing to Labor, increasing their 2PP lead to 58 percent, a rise of two percent. Labor's primary vote was up three to 51 percent, with the Liberals down two to 34 percent. Rudd extended his lead by two percent to 50 percent, with Howard down by two percent to 37 percent.<ref>[http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/files/newspoll-23oct.pdf Newspoll 23/10/2007 PDF]</ref>

===Leaders' debate===
A debate between the Leader of the Opposition and the Prime Minister, under the moderation of the [[National Press Club (Australia)|National Press Club]], was shown live on [[ABC TV]], the [[Nine Network]], and [[Sky News Australia]] at 7.30pm on [[21 October]]. Nine averaged 1.42 million viewers while the ABC averaged 907,000 and Sky News averaged 62,000.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/10/22/1192940948751.html|title=Worm wriggles to top of ratings|last=Cooke|first=Dewi|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[22 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> [[David Speers]], Sky News' Political Editor, moderated the debate which was held in the [[Australian_Parliament_House#Layout|Great Hall]] of [[Parliament House, Canberra|Parliament House]]. Steps were taken to ensure the balance of Labor/Liberal supporters was 50/50. At one point, [[Peter Costello]] was reprimanded for interjecting.<ref>http://www.news.com.au/couriermail/story/0,23739,22624834-952,00.html</ref>

The Nine Network, which broadcast the debate as an extended edition of [[60 Minutes (Australia)|60 Minutes]], attracted controversy for its broadcast, which included the use of '[[Worm (marketing)|The Worm]]' despite objections from the Liberal Party and action from the National Press Club to cease its video feed. As a result, the Nine network's feed was cut part of the way into the broadcast, which it replaced with Sky News' coverage.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/10/21/1192940903661.html|title=Howard goes on the offensive|last=Davis|first=Mark|work=[[Sydney Morning Herald]]|date=[[22 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> The Nine television network's live audience, via the worm's average, scored the debate 65 to 29 in Rudd's favour,<ref>http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/10/22/2066389.htm?site=elections/federal/2007</ref> with 6% remaining undecided. Both sides, however, claimed victory.<ref>http://www.news.com.au/story/0,23599,22625345-1702,00.html</ref>

Kevin Rudd used part of the debate to argue that the Liberal Party was being influenced by the [[H. R. Nicholls Society]] to make further reforms to industrial relations, citing [[Nick Minchin]]'s attendance to last year's H. R. Nicholls Society conference, where he told the audience that the coalition "knew its reform to [[WorkChoices]] were not popular but the process of change must continue",<ref>http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/story/0,25197,22567537-11949,00.html</ref> and that "there is still a long way to go... awards, the IR commission, all the rest of it..."<ref>http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/10/14/1192300600890.html</ref> Countering the Liberal Party message that 70 percent of Labor's front bench is made up of former union officials, Rudd stated that 70 percent of Liberal Party ministers were either lawyers or former Liberal Party staffers.<ref>http://www.news.com.au/heraldsun/story/0,21985,22624945-5005961,00.html</ref> According to [[Wendy Allen]], a PhD candidate in linguistics at the [[University of Melbourne]] who is writing a thesis on political discourse, Howard also played into Labor's hands by for the first time referring to economic progress in the past "10 or 20 years", undoing the campaign message that Australia's strong economy is the Coalition's preserve.<ref>http://www.bordermail.com.au/news/bm/national/982632.html</ref>

==Electoral prospects: House of Representatives==
{{see also|Candidates of the Australian federal election, 2007}}
Though the government enjoys a comfortable majority of 27 in the 150-seat [[Australian House of Representatives]], 23 of its MPs will be defending two-party-preferred (2PP) margins of 6% or less in 2007. Labor requires a gain of 16 seats to form a [[majority government]] in the lower house, which corresponds to a [[Mackerras Pendulum|uniform swing]] of 4.8%.

===High-profile candidates===
* Former ABC journalist [[Maxine McKew]] will challenge John Howard in his electorate of [[Division of Bennelong|Bennelong]].<ref name="Maxine McKew">{{cite news |publisher = The Sydney Morning Herald |title = McKew makes presence felt in Bennelong |url = http://www.smh.com.au/news/National/McKew-makes-presence-felt-in-Bennelong/2007/05/01/1177788124316.html |date = [[2007-05-01]] |accessdate = 2007-05-05
}}</ref>

* Union leader [[Bill Shorten]], touted as a future prime minister during the [[Beaconsfield mine collapse]] drama, will contest the safe Labor seat of [[Division of Maribyrnong|Maribyrnong]].

* Former New South Wales Attorney-General [[Bob Debus]] will contest the marginal seat of [[Division of Macquarie|Macquarie]].

* [[ACTU]] Secretary [[Greg Combet]] has been announced as the ALP's candidate for [[Division of Charlton|Charlton]], a safe seat in New South Wales. The preselection received criticism and anger from [[Kelly Hoare]], the MP he replaces, and others within the New South Wales Labor Party. <ref name="Anger at Combet">{{cite news |publisher = The Advertiser |last = Anderson |first = Laura |title = Anger as Combet chases safe seat |url = http://www.news.com.au/adelaidenow/story/0,22606,21673600-910,00.html |date = [[May 5]] [[2007]] |accessdate = 2007-05-05}}</ref>

* [[Division of Corio|Corio]], which is a Labor seat on 5.6 percent, will be contested by Assistant Secretary of the ACTU [[Richard Marles]] as the Labor candidate after incumbent [[Gavan O'Connor]] lost party preselection in March 2007. In October 2007, O'Connor announced he would run as an independent candidate. 

* [[Nicole Cornes]], a journalist for the ''[[The Advertiser (Australia)|Sunday Mail]]'' and the wife of media personality and ex-footballer [[Graham Cornes]] is the ALP's candidate for [[Division of Boothby|Boothby]], a South Australian seat which Liberal MP [[Andrew Southcott]] currently holds by a margin of 5.4%. 

* [[Mia Handshin]], a Young South Australian of the Year, a journalist for ''[[The Advertiser (Australia)|The Advertiser]]'' and a small business operator, will contest another South Australian seat, [[Division of Sturt|Sturt]] in which she resides. Sturt is currently represented by a minister, [[Christopher Pyne]], who holds the seat with a 6.8% margin. <ref name="Union heavies">{{cite news |publisher = The Advertiser |title = Union heavies join Labor's star recruits |url = http://www.news.com.au/adelaidenow/story/0,22606,21673601-910,00.html |date = [[May 5]] [[2007]] |accessdate = 2007-05-05}}</ref> 

* Former Australian soldier and lawyer [[Mike Kelly (politician)|Mike Kelly]] will contest the [[bellwether]] seat of [[Eden-Monaro]] in regional NSW. 

* Former [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]] weather reporter [[Mike Bailey (weatherman)|Mike Bailey]] will contest [[Division of North Sydney|North Sydney]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/05/22/1179601365523.html?page=fullpage|title=Weatherman Bailey quits ABC for politics|last=Coorey|first=Philip|work=Sydney Morning Herald|date=[[22 May]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

* Former diplomat, [[Bruce Haigh]] announced recently that he will be standing as an Independent candidate for the seat of [[Division of Parkes|Parkes]]. Haigh was embroiled in controversy when he used his diplomatic immunity to help black activists flee repression in South Africa.<ref>[http://www.brucehaigh.com.au Bruce Haigh campaign site]</ref>

* Former journalist, academic and media personality Patricia Petersen will contest the seat of [[Division of Warringah|Warringah]] (currently held by [[Tony Abbott]]).

===Redistribution===

An electoral [[Redistribution (Australia)|redistribution]] completed in [[Mackerras federal election pendulum, 2006|September 2006]] by the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] will increase the size of the Queensland delegation by one, at the expense of New South Wales. The western New South Wales seat of [[Division of Gwydir|Gwydir]] will be abolished and a new seat of [[Division of Flynn|Flynn]], based around [[Gladstone, Queensland|Gladstone]], created in its place. Both the old seat and the new are safe for the National Party.

The other major change sees boundaries shift for Liberal-held [[Division of Macquarie|Macquarie]] and Labor-held [[Division of Parramatta|Parramatta]]: both are now notionally held by the opposing party. A number of other seats were also substantially changed, including [[Division of Parkes|Parkes]], [[Division of Farrer|Farrer]], [[Division of Calare|Calare]], [[Division of Greenway|Greenway]] and [[Division of Hughes|Hughes]].

===Marginal seats===

Labor and the Coalition each hold 23 [[marginal seat]]s: seats with 2PP margins of 6% or less. Since the 2006 redistribution, Labor-held Parramatta is notionally a Liberal marginal and Liberal-held Macquarie is notionally a Labor marginal.

The marginal seats of [[Division of Makin|Makin]] (South Australia) and [[Division of Cowan|Cowan]] (Western Australia), held on 2PP margins of less than 1% by the Liberals and Labor respectively, will be especially closely-watched, with sitting MPs [[Trish Draper]] and [[Graham Edwards (politician)|Graham Edwards]] retiring at the election. In the south-eastern New South Wales seat of [[Division of Eden-Monaro|Eden-Monaro]], Liberal [[Gary Nairn]] will be defending the so-called [[bellwether]] electorate for the government with a 2PP margin of 3.3%. The Liberal electorate of [[Division of Lindsay|Lindsay]], with a 2PP margin of 2.9% will be another seat which will be hotly contested due to popular member [[Jackie Kelly]]'s announcement that she will not be recontesting the seat.

Currently polling indicates that the two [[Tasmania]]n marginal seats of [[Division of Bass|Bass]] and [[Division of Braddon|Braddon]], both in the state's north, are likely to return to the ALP at this election. Both seats were lost by the ALP to the government at the last election, with commentators associating this with Mark Latham's forestry policy and its lack of popularity in these seats.

