{{Australian Senators}}

==Electoral prospects: Senate==
A total of 36 senators are not up for re-election: 19 from the Coalition, 14 from Labor, two [[Australian Greens]] and one [[Family First]]. The Coalition needs to win 20 of the 40 contested Senate seats to maintain its Senate majority, while Labor would need to win 25 seats to have a Senate majority in its own right. A Labor majority would require a preferred vote of over 57% in five of the six states; this would be a record for Labor, surpassing the 55.09 percent it recorded in the [[Australian federal election, 1943|1943 election]]. On recent polling, it appears likely that the balance of power will revert to the minor parties.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/06/26/1182623896172.html|title=Govt warns against Greens after poll|date=[[26 June]] [[2007]]|work=Sydney Morning Herald|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

The minor parties and independents with a chance of winning Senate seats are the [[Australian Greens]], [[Family First]] and [[Australian Democrats]], and independents [[Nick Xenophon]] and [[Pauline Hanson]].<ref>[http://www.roymorgan.com/news/polls/2007/4227/ Finding No. 4227 - Latest Morgan Senate Poll: October 18, 2007]</ref> <ref name=nopokies>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/10/12/2057774.htm|title=No Pokies MP odds-on for Senate seat|date=[[12 October]] [[2007]]|work=ABC News Online|accessdate=2007-10-22}}<br>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/10/17/1192300859185.html|title=Recognise these men? They may hold balance of power|work=The Age|date=[[18 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> Each party's success will largely depend on securing favourable preference flows from the major parties and assorted micro-parties. While the Coalition is expected to preference Family First ahead of the Greens, the battle to secure Labor's preferences will be fierce.

The [[Australian Democrats]], who were the minor party with the most Senators from 1978 to 2005, have been widely predicted to lose all their seats.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/news/national/last-chance-for-democrats-to-assert-their-relevance/2007/10/17/1192300859173.html|title=Last chance for Democrats to assert their relevance|last=Dubecki|first=Larissa|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[18 October]] [[2007]]}}</ref> All four Democrat seats are up for re-election, and two incumbents, [[Andrew Murray (politician)|Andrew Murray]] and [[Natasha Stott Despoja]], have stated that they will not seek a fresh term.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/09/22/1189881833182.html|title=So, is the party over?|last=Koutsoukis|first=Jason|work=The Age|date=[[23 September]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>  

Coalition senators [[Paul Calvert]], [[Rod Kemp]], [[Sandy Macdonald]] and [[Kay Patterson]]; and Labor senator [[Robert Ray (Australian politician)|Robert Ray]] will not contest the 2007 election.

===High-profile candidates===
(<!-- Please note that, to avoid the appearance of promoting candidates here, this list is sorted -->Alphabetically by surname.<!-- Please keep it that way -->)

* Former Queensland politician [[Pauline Hanson]] announced recently that she will be standing for the Senate in Queensland. <ref> [http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/08/15/2005271.htm "Hanson Launches new political party."], ''[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]]'', [[August 15]], [[2007]]/ Accessed [[August}}, [[2007]].</ref> Recent polling indicates her primary vote to be around 7.5 percent, but it is expected that she will stuggle to get the preferences required to gain a seat.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/10/19/1192301026185.html|title=Hanson attracting 7.5% support in Qld|work=Sydney Morning Herald|date=[[19 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

* Author and broadcaster Dr [[Karl Kruszelnicki]] is running for the Senate in New South Wales for the [[Climate Change Coalition]], occupying second place on the party's ticket in that state behind [[Patrice Newell]].

* [[Andrew Wilkie]] has been pre-selected as second on the ticket for the [[Tasmanian Greens]] behind Senator [[Bob Brown]]. He is a former [[intelligence officer]] who resigned from the [[Office of National Assessments (Australia)|Office of National Assessments]] (ONA) over his concerns about government use of intelligence information in building a case for the [[2003 invasion of Iraq]]. In the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]] he polled 16% for the [[Australian Greens|Greens]] in Prime Minister [[John Howard]]'s electorate of [[Division of Bennelong|Bennelong]].

* [[Nick Xenophon]], a [[No Pokies]] MP in the [[Parliament of South Australia]], resigned to run for a South Australian Senate seat. He attracted a fifth of the statewide upper house vote (20.5 percent) at the [[South Australian general election, 2006|2006 state election]], and it is widely predicted that he will win a Senate seat<ref name=nopokies />, and possibly hold the [[Balance of power (politics)|balance of power]].<ref>http://www.news.com.au/adelaidenow/story/0,22606,22571626-5006301,00.html</ref> His platform will consist of anti-gambling, pro-[[consumer protection]], attention to the water crisis, opposition against what he calls a decrease in state rights, and opposition to [[WorkChoices]].<ref>http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/story/0,25197,22567900-5006787,00.html</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/10/11/1191696082652.html|title=Fearful of Xenophon in Senate|last=Debelle|first=Penelope|work=The Age|date=[[12 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

